{"reference":"1 Kings 21","book":"1 Kings","chapter":21,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Naboth's vineyard and royal power","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter presents Ahab's acquisition of Naboth's inherited vineyard as the result of a deliberate abuse of power. After Naboth refuses to surrender it, Jezebel arranges false accusations, Naboth is executed, and Ahab takes possession. Elijah's confrontation makes the charge explicit: the royal house is condemned for murder and seizure, not simply for Ahab's desire for a nearby vineyard. The final scene does not erase that judgment; it records that Ahab humbles himself and that the announced disaster is delayed until his son's days.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 21:1-29"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_021_8ef54adcb8e08b61125a645a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Ahab beyond this chapter","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"An inscription known as the Kurkh Monolith names Ahab in a coalition at Qarqar, an event not narrated in 1 Kings. It independently places an Israelite king in the regional politics of the ninth century, while not confirming every detail or theological claim made in the book's account of Ahab.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 16:29-34","1 Kings 17:1-24","1 Kings 18:1-46","1 Kings 19:1-21","1 Kings 20:1-43","1 Kings 22:1-40"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_021_87de6b7e95f82a3dc3499bb7"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}